          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em finite dimensional Hilbert space
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/underlying"} underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+vector+space"} finite-dimensional vector space
          %p
            Since the completeness-condition on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            is automatically satisfied in finite dimensions, finite-dimensional Hilbert spaces are equivalently
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+dimensional+vector+spaces"} finite dimensional vector spaces
            (usually
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+vector+spaces"} complex vector spaces
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+vector+spaces"} real vector spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermitian+form"} Hermitian
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+product"} inner product
            = succeed "." do
              %em Hermtian inner product spaces
          %p
            Finite-dimensional Hilbert spaces are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces+of+quantum+states"} spaces of quantum states
            of principal interest in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory"} quantum information theory
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computation"} quantum computation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            more broadly they appear in discussion of internal degrees of freedom (such as
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin"} spin
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+space"} compact
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+spaces"} phase spaces
            (cf. eg. the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization+of+the+2-sphere"} geometric quantization of the 2-sphere
            or generally the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbit+method"} orbit method
          %p
            The fact that they form a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dagger-compact+category"} dagger-compact category
            \&amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#Selinger12"} cf.
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#AbramskyCoecke04"} Abramsky &amp; Coecke 2004 p 10
            = succeed "-formulation" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagram"} string diagram
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory+via+dagger-compact+categories"} quantum information theory via dagger-compact categories
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory+via+dagger-compact+categories"} quantum information theory via dagger-compact categories
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+vector+space"} finite-dimensional vector space
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+abelian+category"} finite abelian category
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+R.+Halmos"}> Paul R. Halmos
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Finite-Dimensional Hilbert Spaces
                %strong 77
                5 (1970) 457-464 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2307/2317378"}> doi:10.2307/2317378
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.jstor.org/stable/2317378"} jstor:2317378
            %li#AbramskyCoecke04
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Samson+Abramsky"}> Samson Abramsky
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bob+Coecke"} Bob Coecke
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A categorical semantics of quantum protocols
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0402130"} arXiv:quant-ph/0402130
            %li#Selinger12
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Selinger"}> Peter Selinger
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Finite dimensional Hilbert spaces are complete for dagger compact closed categories
                %strong 8
                3 (2012) lmcs:1086 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1207.6972"}> arXiv:1207.6972
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2168/LMCS-8(3:6)2012"} doi:10.2168/LMCS-8(3:6)2012
            %li#Landsman17
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaas+Landsman"}> Klaas Landsman
                , §A.1 in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Foundations of quantum theory – From classical concepts to Operator algebras
                = succeed "," do
